@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Variant — same collaboration shown black-box vs expanded.
'   usecase:      Note abstraction level: black-box hides internals, expanded exposes them.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       business-process
' ============================================================================
title Example BPMN Collaboration - Black-box vs Expanded (Variant)

' Same Buyer/Seller exchange shown two ways.
' Left: black-box pools — empty rectangles, only message flows visible.
' Right: expanded pools — internal process exposed.
' Choose black-box when you only control the conversation contract.
' Choose expanded when you must model each participant's internal logic.

' --- Part 1: Black-box ----------------------------------------------------
rectangle "**Buyer (black-box)**" as BB1
rectangle "**Seller (black-box)**" as BB2
BB1 ..> BB2 : "Order"
BB2 ..> BB1 : "Goods"

note top of BB1
  <b>Black-box pool</b>
  Internal process hidden.
  Useful when the participant's
  internals are out of scope or
  owned by another team.
end note

' --- Part 2: Expanded -----------------------------------------------------
rectangle "**Buyer (expanded)**" as EB1 {
  usecase " " as Bs
  rectangle "Submit Order" as Bt1
  rectangle "Receive Goods" as Bt2
  usecase " " as Be
  Bs --> Bt1
  Bt1 --> Bt2
  Bt2 --> Be
}

rectangle "**Seller (expanded)**" as EB2 {
  usecase " " as Ss
  rectangle "Accept Order" as St1
  rectangle "Ship Goods" as St2
  usecase " " as Se
  Ss --> St1
  St1 --> St2
  St2 --> Se
}

Bt1 ..> St1 : "Order"
St2 ..> Bt2 : "Goods"

note top of EB1
  <b>Expanded pool</b>
  Internal tasks visible.
  Use when you own/need to reason
  about a participant's internals.
end note

note bottom of EB2
  <b>Abstraction trade-off</b>
  Black-box: lighter, focused on
  the contract. Expanded: heavier,
  exposes sequence flow + events.
  Mix freely in one collaboration.
end note

@enduml
